+# --- the predictions gate: refuse a section this hive cannot read --------------
+# The gate used to be a human reading a `## Predictions` heading while hive-score
+# parsed bullet LABELS. Two faithful readings of one protocol produced two
+# dialects, the scorer understood one of them, and an entire tenant project's
+# first wave scored `unpredicted` with no error printed anywhere — the brief
+# recorded 6/6 against an actual 4 of 6 (retro 2026-07-29 D1). The prose half of
+# that repair then failed again within a day, in an order written by an author
+# reading the corrected protocol. So the check moves here, into the tool that
+# already opens the file, and it calls hive-score's OWN parser (predictions_parse,
+# hive-common.sh) rather than a second copy of the regexes — a gate that can
+# disagree with the scorer is precisely the defect being repaired.
+#
+# It runs BEFORE the throttle's board reads, before the pin, before the clones and
+# the wrapper, and long before any emit: a refusal here has changed nothing.
+#
+# What refuses is narrow, and the boundary matters more than the check:
+#   absent       -> LAUNCH. No section at all is a gap hive-score already records
+#                   as a metric. An order the operator deliberately launches
+#                   unpredicted must never need --anyway.
+#   declared     -> LAUNCH. The protocol's one-line deliberately-unpredicted
+#                   disposition is a legitimate answer, not a missing one.
+#   partial      -> LAUNCH, loudly. Partial coverage is a number the instrument
+#                   already reports; refusing it would be a new gate, not a repair.
+#   full         -> LAUNCH, silently.
+#   unparseable  -> REFUSE. A section is there, the author meant it, and nothing
+#                   downstream can read it. This is the only shape that is
+#                   information already lost at launch time.
